      "writtenOn": "2022-03-17T17:23:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "nodelabel\n\nThis helps differentiate from the label property (which is EEPROM_CBI in your example).",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 32,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 32,
        "endChar": 24
      },
      "revId": "37fefd623e0abac8a266a361c86d72148dbf7e7c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e4bfa91f_c7f4b034",
        "filename": "docs/zephyr/zephyr_eeprom.md",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 55,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-03-17T17:23:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The current requirement is that you define an alias node called \"gpio-cbi-wp\" that points to the GPIO used as the write protect.\n\nExample here:\nhttps://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec/+/bb733b03/zephyr/projects/skyrim/skyrim.dts#9\n\nAlso, you should note that the \"wp-gpios\" property should not be used.  \n\n\"wp-gpios\" - Specifies the GPIO output signal from the SoC connected to the EEPROM write protect pin.  Zephyr uses this to automatically control write protection during write and erase operations.\n\nHowever on Chromebooks, the write protect signal is controlled by the Google Security Chip.  gpio-cbi-wb specifies the GPIO input signal to the SoC, used by the EC application to monitor the write protect state.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 53,
        "startChar": 59,
        "endLine": 55,
        "endChar": 0
      },
      "revId": "37fefd623e0abac8a266a361c86d72148dbf7e7c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "89ae8ff2_10cea03b",
        "filename": "docs/zephyr/zephyr_eeprom.md",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 71,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-03-17T17:23:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You could point to the I2C document which has details for doing an I2C bus scan.  This can be used to verify the EEPROM device can be accessed.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 71,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 71,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "37fefd623e0abac8a266a361c86d72148dbf7e7c",
